{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-09-21T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"No other city is nearly as romanticised as Paris. Yet the French capital's history has not always been as rosy as the sunset glowing behind the Eiffel Tower. Built for the 1889 Exposition Universelle, or the World's Fair, which marked the centennial of the start of the French Revolution, the tower was originally intended to serve as the fair's entrance centrepiece. Designed by engineer Gustave Eiffel and his team, the iron landmark rose 300 metres above the Champ de Mars and became the tallest structure in the world at the time.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ParisSunset_ROW1775373883_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ParisSunset_ROW1775373883_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Eiffel Tower at sunset, Paris, France","Copyright":"© Alexander Spatari/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260920_ParisSunset\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Paris&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260920_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260920_ParisSunset"},"Ssd":"20260920","Name":"ParisSunset","Market":"ROW","Hash":"1775373883","FullDateString":"20. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Think you know what a desert looks like? Lençóis Maranhenses National Park might change your mind. Along Brazil's Maranhão coast, huge white dunes sit beside freshwater lagoons, creating a landscape that changes with the seasons. Established as a national park in 1981 and recognised as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2024, it covers more than 156,000 hectares (about 386,887 acres) and stretches along 80 kilometres of coastline. The dunes are shaped by prevailing winds, while seasonal rains collect in the hollows between them. Some lagoons last only through the wet season, while others remain year-round, including Lagoa da Esperança.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.Lencois_ROW1554257872_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.Lencois_ROW1554257872_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Lençóis Maranhenses National Park, Brazil","Copyright":"© Ignacio Palacios/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260919_Lencois\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Lencois+Maranhenses+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260919_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260919_Lencois"},"Ssd":"20260919","Name":"Lencois","Market":"ROW","Hash":"1554257872","FullDateString":"19. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"A long, winding road cuts through rocks more than 300 million years old in England's Peak District. As if that geological timescale were not remarkable enough, the road passes through Winnats Pass, a narrow limestone gorge whose towering cliffs reveal the ancient seas that once covered this part of Britain.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.WinnatsPassPeak_ROW1327716506_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.WinnatsPassPeak_ROW1327716506_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Winnats Pass, Peak District National Park, England","Copyright":"© Daniel_Kay/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260918_WinnatsPassPeak\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Peak+District+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260918_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260918_WinnatsPassPeak"},"Ssd":"20260918","Name":"WinnatsPassPeak","Market":"ROW","Hash":"1327716506","FullDateString":"18. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Each year, late summer and early autumn bring one of France's most anticipated traditions: the 'vendanges,' the grape-picking season. The timing varies from one wine region to another and depends on weather conditions, sun exposure and the ripeness of the fruit. For many vineyards, harvest is still a hands-on effort, with teams carefully selecting bunches destined for some of the country's most celebrated wines.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.Santenay_ROW6963784310_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.Santenay_ROW6963784310_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Sorine windmill and vineyards, Santenay wine region, Côte de Beaune, Burgundy, France","Copyright":"© Marco Bottigelli/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260917_Santenay\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Santenay+wine+region&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260917_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260917_Santenay"},"Ssd":"20260917","Name":"Santenay","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6963784310","FullDateString":"17. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Two polar bear cubs pick their way across the snow in Svalbard, a remote Norwegian archipelago of glaciers, mountains and frozen fjords deep in the Arctic Ocean. This wild landscape is part of the Barents Sea region, home to one of the world's largest polar bear populations, with around 2,600 bears.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.IcyCubs_ROW6681401044_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.IcyCubs_ROW6681401044_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Polar bear cubs playing in Svalbard, Norway","Copyright":"© Danny Green/Nature Picture Library","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260916_IcyCubs\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Polar+bear&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260916_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260916_IcyCubs"},"Ssd":"20260916","Name":"IcyCubs","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6681401044","FullDateString":"16. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Evolution occasionally favours maximalism. Just look at these red-and-green macaws with their scarlet plumage, vivid green wings and blue flight feathers. Their lives unfold across tropical South America, in a routine that connects fruit, seeds and soil in ways that are easy to miss from the ground.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.RedMacawsFlight_ROW6429842138_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.RedMacawsFlight_ROW6429842138_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Red-and-green macaws","Copyright":"© markes51/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260915_RedMacawsFlight\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Red-and-green+macaw&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260915_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260915_RedMacawsFlight"},"Ssd":"20260915","Name":"RedMacawsFlight","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6429842138","FullDateString":"15. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In today's image, kochia plants spread across the landscape in China in shades of green and deep red. Native to parts of Eurasia, kochia was introduced to the United States in the late 1800s as an ornamental plant and a source of material for broom making. Its ability to withstand drought, heat, saline soils and low rainfall helped it spread across the Great Plains and much of the American West.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.KochiaChina_ROW6162442762_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.KochiaChina_ROW6162442762_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Field of kochia plants, China","Copyright":"© lingqi xie/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260914_KochiaChina\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Kochia+plant&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260914_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260914_KochiaChina"},"Ssd":"20260914","Name":"KochiaChina","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6162442762","FullDateString":"14. Sept rrrr"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Forget rolling vineyards—around Stuttgart in Germany, the vines take the uphill route, climbing steep terraces above the Neckar valley and weaving between dry-stone walls. These vineyards are a defining feature of the city's landscape, with some of the steepest plots requiring extensive handwork because machinery is difficult to use. Local winegrowers, known as Wengerter, tend the vines on slopes that can reach gradients of 50 per cent. Traditional varieties such as Trollinger and Riesling are closely associated with the region and remain widely grown today.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NeckarVineyards_ROW5359254474_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NeckarVineyards_ROW5359254474_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Sunset over the vineyards of Steinhaldenfeld, Neckar valley, Stuttgart, Germany","Copyright":"© Cyril Gosselin/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260927_NeckarVineyards\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Neckar+valley&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260927_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260927_NeckarVineyards"},"Ssd":"20260927","Name":"NeckarVineyards","Market":"ROW","Hash":"5359254474","FullDateString":"27. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What do a bear's ears, a house on fire and a road packed with hairpin turns have in common? They're all part of the story of America's public lands in southeastern Utah. Bears Ears National Monument protects important cultural and natural resources, including archaeological sites that reflect thousands of years of human history. Its name comes from two buttes that rise side by side and resemble a bear's ears.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.BearsEars_ROW3487899068_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.BearsEars_ROW3487899068_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Cedar Mesa and Muley Point near Bears Ears National Monument, Utah, United States","Copyright":"© Jeff Clay/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260926_BearsEars\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Bears+Ears+National+Monument&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260926_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260926_BearsEars"},"Ssd":"20260926","Name":"BearsEars","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3487899068","FullDateString":"26. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Most rivers spend their lives flowing around obstacles. The Sharavathi River reaches the edge of a cliff and simply jumps. At Jog Falls in Karnataka, the river plunges 253 metres, making it the third-highest waterfall in India.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.JogFalls2026_ROW3282923682_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.JogFalls2026_ROW3282923682_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Jog Falls, Sharavathi River, Karnataka, India","Copyright":"© Amith Nag Photography/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260925_JogFalls2026\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Jog+Falls+India&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260925_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260925_JogFalls2026"},"Ssd":"20260925","Name":"JogFalls2026","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3282923682","FullDateString":"25. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Castelmezzano, in the province of Potenza in Basilicata, Italy, is hidden from view until you reach it. You arrive through a tunnel and as you emerge, the village suddenly appears, wedged among the sandstone pinnacles of the Lucanian Dolomites. The houses seem to climb over one another at around 750 metres above sea level, connected by steps and narrow lanes that rise towards the remains of the Norman fortress.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.CastelmezzanoItaly_ROW3044667748_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.CastelmezzanoItaly_ROW3044667748_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Castelmezzano, Basilicata, Italy","Copyright":"© Sean Pavone/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260924_CastelmezzanoItaly\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Castelmezzano&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260924_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260924_CastelmezzanoItaly"},"Ssd":"20260924","Name":"CastelmezzanoItaly","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3044667748","FullDateString":"24. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"A round door, a bright green frame and a hillside that looks straight out of Middle-earth—welcome to Hobbiton Movie Set in Matamata, New Zealand. Built in 1999 for 'The Lord of the Rings,' the original set featured 39 temporary Hobbit Holes across a 4.9-hectare site. After filming ended, the set was dismantled, but it returned in 2009 for 'The Hobbit' films and was rebuilt as a permanent attraction.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.TolkienWeek_ROW2596246369_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.TolkienWeek_ROW2596246369_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Hobbit house at Hobbiton Movie Set, Matamata, New Zealand","Copyright":"© djr-photography/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260923_TolkienWeek\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Hobbiton+Movie+Set&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260923_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260923_TolkienWeek"},"Ssd":"20260923","Name":"TolkienWeek","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2596246369","FullDateString":"23. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What makes a tree 'quake?' Take a closer look at an aspen leaf. Its flattened stalk catches even a light breeze, making the leaves flutter and giving the tree its name: quaking aspen. Near Guardsman Pass in Utah, United States, these trees form striking groves across the higher mountain slopes, where cooler, moist conditions suit them well.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.FallAspens_ROW2359837997_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.FallAspens_ROW2359837997_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Autumn aspen trees near Guardsman Pass, Utah, United States","Copyright":"© Danita Delimont/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260922_FallAspens\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Aspen+trees&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260922_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260922_FallAspens"},"Ssd":"20260922","Name":"FallAspens","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2359837997","FullDateString":"22. Sept rrrr"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Few animals are as closely tied to the health of coastal ecosystems as the sea otter. In today's image, one grooms its fur in Monterey Bay, California, USA. This behaviour is far more than a daily ritual. Unlike seals and whales, sea otters have little insulating blubber, so they rely on the densest fur of any animal. Careful cleaning traps air within their coat, creating a waterproof layer that helps them stay warm in the cold Pacific.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.GroomingOtter_ROW2104979669_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.GroomingOtter_ROW2104979669_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Sea otter grooming fur, Monterey Bay, California, United States","Copyright":"© Suzi Eszterhas/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260921_GroomingOtter\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Sea+otter&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260921_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260921_GroomingOtter"},"Ssd":"20260921","Name":"GroomingOtter","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2104979669","FullDateString":"21. Sept rrrr"}],"LocStrings":{"LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_IOTD":"Obrázek dne","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_IOTD_LEARN_MORE":"Další informace","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_DONATE_NOW":"Přispět nyní","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_FACT":"Stručné skutečnosti:","LOC_HOMEPAGE_GALLERY_CARD_TITLE":"Nedávné obrázky na domovské stránc